WEBVTT

00:00.050 --> 00:05.990
So now we understand that this idea of a fraction is like parts of a whole.

00:05.990 --> 00:14.180
And to refresh that, we can imagine a baseball player who maybe has three at bats in a single game.

00:14.180 --> 00:20.390
They come to the plate three times and out of those three at bats, out of those three chances to get

00:20.390 --> 00:22.810
a hit, they get a hit two times.

00:22.820 --> 00:28.610
So that means in that particular game, out of three at bats, they had two hits.

00:28.610 --> 00:33.710
Their hit percentage was two thirds or their ratio was two thirds.

00:33.710 --> 00:42.980
Or we could give an example of maybe I have five books on my bookshelf in total and I've read two of

00:42.980 --> 00:43.400
them.

00:43.400 --> 00:50.990
Well, that means I've read 2/5 of the total books that I have, or I'm 2/5 of the way through the books

00:50.990 --> 00:52.580
on my bookshelf.

00:52.580 --> 01:00.180
So it's this idea of a ratio or a proportion or a relationship between the value and the numerator and

01:00.180 --> 01:02.250
the value in the denominator.

01:02.250 --> 01:09.840
And so we understand that we have this concept of a fraction, but now we want to take that idea of

01:09.840 --> 01:17.460
the fraction as a relationship and expand it to recognize that two fractions can be equivalent even

01:17.460 --> 01:19.830
if they're written differently.

01:19.830 --> 01:28.860
So let's think about here to take another example that I'm driving 945 miles on a road trip, and so

01:28.860 --> 01:33.030
far I've driven 630 of those miles.

01:33.030 --> 01:38.430
So 630 miles driven so far out of 945 total miles.

01:38.430 --> 01:40.800
This is the part of the whole.

01:40.800 --> 01:47.220
And what I want to figure out is the proportion of miles that I've driven so far written this way.

01:47.250 --> 01:53.130
630 divided by 945 It's really hard for me to interpret what this means.

01:53.130 --> 01:56.010
How far really have I gone so far?

01:56.010 --> 01:59.310
It's really hard to tell because these numbers are so large.

01:59.310 --> 02:07.530
So what I'd like to do instead is see if I can simplify this fraction into a set of smaller numbers

02:07.530 --> 02:09.990
where the numerator and denominator are smaller numbers.

02:09.990 --> 02:13.920
I'm looking for an equivalent fraction that's easier for me to interpret.

02:13.920 --> 02:19.980
And the way that we're going to do that is by looking for common factors between the numerator and denominator

02:19.980 --> 02:21.510
that we can cancel.

02:21.510 --> 02:27.720
Now, when we have big numbers like this, oftentimes the easiest way to do that is to find the prime

02:27.720 --> 02:31.800
factorization of both the numerator and the denominator.

02:31.800 --> 02:37.230
So let's start with here 630 and look for its prime factorization.

02:37.230 --> 02:41.250
So we're looking for any factor of 630 that we can pull out.

02:41.280 --> 02:46.500
We may think first to divide this by ten because the number ends in zero.

02:46.500 --> 02:49.080
So we know that it's going to be divisible by ten.

02:49.080 --> 02:56.160
So we could rewrite 630 as ten times 63 and use that as our starting point.

02:56.190 --> 02:58.950
Then we know that ten is two times five.

02:58.980 --> 03:02.010
So we could write that as two times five.

03:02.040 --> 03:05.220
Now we have two prime factors, two and five.

03:05.220 --> 03:08.100
We can't break those down any further.

03:08.100 --> 03:13.740
But 63 we know we can write as seven times nine and then nine.

03:13.740 --> 03:16.620
We know we can write as three times three.

03:16.620 --> 03:22.740
And now we've finished our prime factorization because the end of each branch is a prime number.

03:22.740 --> 03:25.980
We have two, five, seven, three and three.

03:26.010 --> 03:33.000
Those are all prime numbers, so we can't take any branch any further, which means the prime factorization

03:33.000 --> 03:40.290
here of 630 is two times three times three times five times seven.

03:40.290 --> 03:43.530
We just wrote the factors in ascending order.

03:43.560 --> 03:48.990
Now let's look at 945 and its prime factorization.

03:49.230 --> 03:55.560
So we might think that our first step is to divide this by five because we can see that 945 ends in

03:55.560 --> 03:55.800
five.

03:55.800 --> 03:57.420
So we know it's divisible by five.

03:57.450 --> 03:59.250
That's something we can pull out right away.

03:59.250 --> 04:03.960
And if we do that, then we take 945 divided by five and we get.

04:04.510 --> 04:05.990
189 now.

04:06.010 --> 04:06.790
189.

04:06.790 --> 04:08.770
We can divide by three.

04:08.950 --> 04:13.030
If we divide 189 by three, we get 63.

04:13.150 --> 04:20.080
And then we know that 63 is seven times nine and then nine we can break down into three times three.

04:20.080 --> 04:26.050
And now we've reached the end of each branch because each branch ends in a prime number.

04:26.050 --> 04:27.970
We know we can't go any further.

04:27.970 --> 04:30.100
So there's our prime factorization.

04:30.100 --> 04:33.460
If we write the factors in ascending order, we get three times.

04:33.460 --> 04:37.770
Three times three times five times seven.

04:37.780 --> 04:45.400
Now we're looking for factors in the numerator and denominator that we can cancel when we have a factor

04:45.400 --> 04:48.310
that exists in both the numerator and the denominator.

04:48.310 --> 04:53.560
We can cancel those factors against each other without changing the value of the fraction.

04:53.560 --> 04:59.140
So to start with here, we can see that we have a factor of three in the numerator and a factor of three

04:59.170 --> 05:00.190
in the denominator.

05:00.190 --> 05:06.620
We can cancel those out and we haven't changed the ratio of the original fraction or the proportion

05:06.620 --> 05:12.050
of the original fraction because three divided by three if we just look at these factors by themselves,

05:12.050 --> 05:14.900
three divided by three is the same as one.

05:14.900 --> 05:21.080
So when we cancel these out, it's just like canceling out a factor of one and we're not changing the

05:21.080 --> 05:23.570
actual value of the fraction so we can keep going.

05:23.600 --> 05:27.380
And we can see here that we have another factor of three that we can cancel.

05:27.380 --> 05:31.740
We have a factor of five, we can cancel and a factor of seven we can cancel.

05:31.760 --> 05:38.120
Now, we can't cancel this three here because it doesn't have a matching three in the numerator.

05:38.120 --> 05:41.270
We have to have a pair of factors to cancel.

05:41.270 --> 05:45.920
And once we've used that factor to cancel, we can't then use it again.

05:45.920 --> 05:52.850
So what's left is two divided by 3 or 2 thirds or two over three.

05:52.880 --> 06:03.110
We see that here, which means that two thirds is an equivalent fraction to 630 divided by 945.

06:03.140 --> 06:06.290
These two fractions mean exactly the same thing.

06:06.290 --> 06:10.340
They represent exactly the same proportion or exactly the same ratio.

06:10.370 --> 06:16.250
It's just that this fraction is written in a much simpler way and that's what we want to do here.

06:16.250 --> 06:22.790
We want to be able to simplify fractions or reduce fractions to their lowest terms so that they're easier

06:22.790 --> 06:30.200
to interpret because when we're on a 945 mile road trip and we've so far gone 630 miles, it might be

06:30.200 --> 06:33.380
hard for us to interpret how far we've actually gone.

06:33.410 --> 06:38.780
But if we can simplify that fraction and we can say instead that we're two thirds of the way there,

06:38.780 --> 06:43.430
we have one third of the trip left, then that's much easier to understand.

06:43.430 --> 06:48.710
And if we broke that down maybe into hours, if we know that this is going to be, let's say, a 12

06:48.710 --> 06:54.020
hour trip, then it'd be easier for us to figure out that we're eight hours of the way in and we have

06:54.020 --> 06:55.640
four hours left.

06:55.670 --> 07:00.950
We're much more likely to be able to determine that when we're looking at this two thirds fraction rather

07:00.950 --> 07:03.860
than this 630 divided by 945.

07:03.860 --> 07:06.980
So that's why we simplify by fractions.

07:07.010 --> 07:11.660
Now that being said, going back to canceling these factors here, we want to make an important point

07:11.660 --> 07:15.500
because this is often easily misunderstood.

07:15.530 --> 07:22.550
But when we have a fraction with the same numerator and denominator, we might be tempted to think that

07:22.550 --> 07:24.050
we can cancel.

07:24.640 --> 07:31.020
These factors and that the result here is zero because both threes go away.

07:31.030 --> 07:32.680
But that's actually not the case.

07:32.680 --> 07:34.150
This is not equal to zero.

07:34.150 --> 07:39.570
Instead, three over three is equal to one.

07:39.580 --> 07:45.550
And in fact any fraction where the numerator and denominator are equivalent, that fraction simplifies

07:45.550 --> 07:46.300
to one.

07:46.300 --> 07:52.000
And the reason is because this three over three right here, we can actually rewrite that as three times

07:52.000 --> 07:55.030
one divided by three times one.

07:55.210 --> 08:00.160
And of course, if that's the case, we could cancel the threes for sure.

08:00.160 --> 08:05.590
But then we're left with one divided by one and we know that one divided by one is one.

08:05.590 --> 08:11.560
We should also know that three divided by three is one because three goes into three one time or one

08:11.560 --> 08:13.420
goes into one one time.

08:13.420 --> 08:17.020
So when we have a fraction like this, the result is one, not zero.

08:17.020 --> 08:21.700
And remember here, when we were canceling factors, we said that this three divided by three was like

08:21.700 --> 08:26.810
canceling out a 1 or 5 divided by five was like canceling out a one.

08:26.810 --> 08:31.670
We have to remember that when we have a fraction like this, it doesn't just disappear, it becomes

08:31.670 --> 08:32.360
one.

08:32.360 --> 08:37.790
So let's look at a couple more examples with equivalent fractions here.

08:37.790 --> 08:42.110
We know that 30 divided by 45 is the same as two thirds.

08:42.110 --> 08:48.200
If we want to show that, if we want to prove that that's true, what we could do is rewrite this left

08:48.200 --> 08:52.100
hand side by factoring out a common factor of 15.

08:52.130 --> 09:02.390
We know that 30 is the same as 15 times two, and we know that 45 is the same as 15 times three.

09:02.600 --> 09:10.250
So if we rewrote 30 over 45 as this fraction, then we could cancel the fifteenths.

09:10.250 --> 09:17.000
And you can see here that we're left with just the two thirds and that's how we know that 30 over 45

09:17.030 --> 09:19.850
is equivalent to two thirds or two over three.

09:19.850 --> 09:29.750
Alternatively, we could start with this two thirds and we could multiply it by 15 over 15 and that

09:29.750 --> 09:39.470
would give us two times 15 is 30 and three times 15 is 45 and we would get to 30 over 45 here from two

09:39.470 --> 09:45.110
over three just by multiplying by 15 over 15, which is the same as multiplying by one.

09:45.110 --> 09:49.640
Because remember, any fraction where the numerator and denominator are equal is equal to one.

09:49.640 --> 09:54.260
So really we're just multiplying this by one written in a different way.

09:54.260 --> 09:58.490
So that's two different ways to show that fractions are equivalent.

09:58.520 --> 10:02.750
We'll also be looking much more at fraction multiplication in a later lesson.

10:02.750 --> 10:08.240
But for now we just kind of want to understand this idea of fractions being equivalent.

10:08.270 --> 10:13.550
Let's look at one more example here and try to figure out equivalent fractions from yet a different

10:13.550 --> 10:14.600
angle here.

10:14.600 --> 10:19.910
We're saying that three divided by five is equal to something divided by ten.

10:19.940 --> 10:24.360
How do we figure out what that something is if we know these fractions are equivalent?

10:24.380 --> 10:28.310
Well, the way to do it is to figure out how we got from 5 to 10.

10:28.310 --> 10:32.530
We started with a denominator of five and ended up with a denominator of ten.

10:32.540 --> 10:34.670
Well, how do we get from 5 to 10?

10:34.700 --> 10:39.680
We multiply by two because five times two is ten.

10:39.680 --> 10:42.470
So we have to multiply here by two.

10:42.470 --> 10:46.250
And whatever we do to the denominator, we have to do to the numerator.

10:46.250 --> 10:52.700
So because we multiply by two here, we have to multiply by two here and then we can say two times three

10:52.730 --> 10:54.200
is six.

10:54.290 --> 11:03.890
And so our result here is six, which means that three divided by five has to be equal to six divided

11:03.890 --> 11:04.700
by ten.

11:04.700 --> 11:11.360
And then we can say that three divided by five and six divided by ten are equivalent fractions.

11:11.390 --> 11:16.250
We can prove that even further if we try to break down six over ten.

11:16.280 --> 11:19.280
Let's rewrite it with its prime factorization.

11:19.280 --> 11:27.170
Well, six divided by ten we know is equal to two times three and two times five.

11:27.170 --> 11:31.100
When we take the prime factorization of six and the prime factorization of ten.

11:31.100 --> 11:37.070
And then we see that we can cancel the common factor of two and we again get back two and we're left

11:37.070 --> 11:42.410
with three divided by five, the fraction we started with originally over here.

11:42.410 --> 11:46.640
So we're going to be doing this all the time as we continue to work with fractions.

11:46.640 --> 11:53.690
The idea is that we want to always be able to simplify a fraction to its lowest terms or to its simplest

11:53.690 --> 11:54.380
form.

11:54.380 --> 12:00.140
Sometimes we're going to be able to do that pretty quickly just by recognizing that we have a common

12:00.140 --> 12:02.000
factor between the numerator and denominator.

12:02.000 --> 12:08.960
We might just be able to look at 30 divided by 45 and immediately recognize the common factor of 15

12:08.960 --> 12:10.130
and pull that out.

12:10.130 --> 12:14.000
So sometimes we're going to be able to do it pretty quickly by sight.

12:14.000 --> 12:18.260
Sometimes when we have something more complicated like this, we're actually going to have to go through

12:18.260 --> 12:23.780
the prime factorization process, write out the prime factors, and then cancel those one at a time

12:23.780 --> 12:24.050
to.

12:24.090 --> 12:26.370
Figure out that this is equal to two thirds.

12:26.370 --> 12:33.450
But either way, that's two methods of simplifying fractions, one just by sight and the other actually

12:33.450 --> 12:35.670
going through the prime factorization process.

12:35.700 --> 12:41.730
Our goal is to be comfortable with both of those so that we can simplify fractions to their lowest terms

12:41.730 --> 12:45.930
whenever we have any kind of complicated fraction like this one.

12:45.930 --> 12:52.050
And then we want to be able to recognize that after we've simplified a fraction, that simplified version

12:52.050 --> 12:55.320
is equivalent always to the fraction we started with.

12:55.350 --> 12:58.620
They represent the same ratio or the same proportion.

12:58.650 --> 13:00.720
They're just written different ways.

13:00.720 --> 13:04.850
So 630 over 945 is equivalent to two thirds.

13:04.860 --> 13:10.920
It's just that two thirds is a simpler expression of that same ratio or that same proportion in the

13:10.920 --> 13:17.160
same way down here, three over five is equivalent to six over ten, but three over five is the most

13:17.160 --> 13:18.420
simplified version.

13:18.450 --> 13:21.780
30 over 45 is equivalent to two thirds.

13:21.780 --> 13:26.320
It's just that two thirds is a simpler version than 30 over 45.

13:26.320 --> 13:32.110
So that's the idea behind simplifying fractions and identifying equivalent fractions.
